WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.308
Checked arithmetic should be designed
before risky arithmetic is performed.

2
00:00:04.388 --> 00:00:07.503
If a result will control allocation,
indexing, authorization,

3
00:00:07.583 --> 00:00:10.007
quota enforcement, parsing, or resource
limits,

4
00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:14.241
the program should verify that the
operation is representable and meaningful before

5
00:00:14.321 --> 00:00:15.359
trusting the result.

6
00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:18.555
Precondition checks are often the clearest
defensive pattern.

7
00:00:18.635 --> 00:00:22.616
Before adding, verify that the left
operand is not above the maximum allowed value

8
00:00:22.696 --> 00:00:23.907
minus the right operand.

9
00:00:23.987 --> 00:00:27.795
Before multiplying, verify that the count
is within a safe relationship to the

10
00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:28.567
element size.

11
00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:32.628
Before converting, verify that the source
value fits the destination type and the

12
00:00:32.708 --> 00:00:33.516
business range.

13
00:00:33.596 --> 00:00:37.808
Postcondition checks can sometimes detect
wraparound when the operation is defined,

14
00:00:37.888 --> 00:00:39.676
especially in unsigned arithmetic,

15
00:00:39.756 --> 00:00:42.468
but they must be appropriate for the type
and operation.

16
00:00:42.548 --> 00:00:45.664
A check that works for addition may not
work for multiplication.

17
00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:49.494
A check that assumes unsigned modulo
behavior should not be applied to signed

18
00:00:49.574 --> 00:00:50.785
overflow after the fact.

19
00:00:50.865 --> 00:00:54.673
Compiler built-ins or vetted helper
functions can improve readability where

20
00:00:54.753 --> 00:00:55.330
available.

21
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.872
A well-named helper can make intent clear:
checked addition for sizes,

22
00:00:58.952 --> 00:01:02.529
checked multiplication for allocation,
checked narrowing conversion,

23
00:01:02.609 --> 00:01:04.398
checked shift, or checked division.

24
00:01:04.478 --> 00:01:07.535
The helper should document accepted
ranges, output behavior,

25
00:01:07.615 --> 00:01:09.000
and how failure is reported.

26
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.388
Saturating arithmetic, clamping,
rejection,

27
00:01:11.468 --> 00:01:13.545
and error returns are different policies.

28
00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:16.452
Clamping a display value may be acceptable
in one context,

29
00:01:16.532 --> 00:01:20.051
while rejecting an allocation request is
the safer behavior in another.

30
00:01:20.131 --> 00:01:22.612
Arithmetic that controls memory, access,
quotas,

31
00:01:22.692 --> 00:01:26.731
or parser advancement often needs
fail-closed behavior rather than a best-effort

32
00:01:26.811 --> 00:01:27.215
result.

33
00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:31.160
Reusable checked-arithmetic patterns
should make the result state explicit.

34
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.164
The caller should know whether the output
was produced, whether it was rejected,

35
00:01:35.244 --> 00:01:38.071
and whether any partially computed value
must be ignored.

36
00:01:38.151 --> 00:01:42.132
Clear result handling prevents safe helper
functions from being misused by later

37
00:01:42.212 --> 00:01:42.500
code.
